WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.790 --> 00:00:05.130
When it comes to good movies, there's a lot
that rides on the actors' performances.

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:08.440
Some roles can't be played without a great
actor at the wheel, and when an actor is at

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:13.530
the height of his or her game, one unforgettable
performance might be enough to nudge an okay

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:16.190
movie up into an award-winning film.

00:00:16.190 --> 00:00:22.140
In the case of these films, these actors performances'
were so strong that they're practically perfect.

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:23.280
Bringing the fury

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:28.090
She may not be the title character, but make
no mistake: the star of Mad Max: Fury Road

00:00:28.090 --> 00:00:32.080
is definitely Charlize Theron's Imperator
Furiosa.

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:36.420
She's the heart and soul of George Miller's
gorgeous post-apocalyptic road war movie.

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:40.590
Of course, it was Miller who gave Theron such
stellar material to work with, but it's not

00:00:40.590 --> 00:00:44.800
hard to imagine the character falling flat
in the hands of a less capable actress.

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:50.280
With Theron behind the wheel, Furiosa is every
bit her name -- a woman of determined fury

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:54.130
hellbent on freeing Immortan Joe's brides
from lives of torment.

00:00:54.130 --> 00:00:59.780
Theron creates a true modern icon, embellishing
her determination with a quiet empathy that

00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:02.720
shines through in some of the film's calmer
moments.

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:08.180
As Furiosa finally achieves her ends and takes
on the big bad, she grunts what's come to

00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:10.460
be the defining line of the film:

00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:11.800
"Remember me?"

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:15.750
We sure do, and will continue to do so for
years to come.

00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:17.680
Making depression hilarious

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:22.930
Ryan Gosling seems to have dedicated much
of his career onscreen to defying expectations.

00:01:22.930 --> 00:01:28.100
After blowing up thanks to a turn in one of
the most beloved romance films of all time,

00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:32.761
Gosling took on a litany of strange, uncomfortable
roles in order to distance himself from the

00:01:32.761 --> 00:01:35.590
traditional career of a leading man in Hollywood.

00:01:35.590 --> 00:01:40.370
This has given us films like The Nice Guys,
which may well mark Gosling's finest hour

00:01:40.370 --> 00:01:41.760
as an actor.

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:46.400
Gosling takes on the role of Holland March,
a depressed alcoholic private investigator

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:49.680
in Los Angeles circa 1977.

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:53.400
March is vulnerable, cowardly, and apt to
self-harm.

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:57.220
There's a shocking degree of drama sprinkled
throughout the performance, considering the

00:01:57.220 --> 00:02:02.259
film is an action comedy, and Gosling sells
March's darkest moments just as expertly as

00:02:02.259 --> 00:02:03.259
his funnier ones.

00:02:03.259 --> 00:02:06.510
At the same time, he's also hysterical in
the movie.

00:02:06.510 --> 00:02:11.470
His may just be one of the great comedic performances
of the decade, with the kind of timing to

00:02:11.470 --> 00:02:14.180
perfectly foil Russell Crowe's straight-man
routine.

00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:15.400
"Munich."

00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:17.720
"What?"

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:22.320
"A guy without his balls … Munich."

00:02:22.329 --> 00:02:26.810
It's a difficult contrast to balance, playing
a life-hating alcoholic who's funny without

00:02:26.810 --> 00:02:29.430
compromising his character's identifiability.

00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:32.919
It takes an actor as talented as Gosling is
to pull it off.

00:02:32.919 --> 00:02:34.230
Burning it down

00:02:34.230 --> 00:02:39.620
Even the best films in the Marvel Cinematic
Universe are often marred by bland, unmemorable

00:02:39.620 --> 00:02:42.969
villains with vaguely-defined goals and personalities.

00:02:42.969 --> 00:02:45.220
There are occasional exceptions like Loki...

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:47.740
"Surprise."

00:02:47.740 --> 00:02:51.469
...but for the most part, Marvel villains
are uninteresting.

00:02:51.469 --> 00:02:56.529
That changed, though, when Michael B. Jordan
hit screens as Erik "Killmonger" Stevens,

00:02:56.529 --> 00:02:59.059
the antagonist of Black Panther.

00:02:59.059 --> 00:03:03.469
Jordan melds swagger with terror, painting
Killmonger as a sympathetic character who's

00:03:03.469 --> 00:03:07.189
just a few adjustments away from being the
film's protagonist.

00:03:07.189 --> 00:03:12.809
His background as a poor kid in Oakland, California
feels all too familiar, so much so that the

00:03:12.809 --> 00:03:16.989
audience is more likely to relate to him than
to T'Challa''s royal upbringing.

00:03:16.989 --> 00:03:22.480
His ultimate inability to distinguish justice
from aimless violence is all the more heartbreaking

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:26.019
because of how close he is to being someone
we love.

00:03:26.019 --> 00:03:30.569
Jordan's charming recklessness and seething
hatred make for a character who feels doomed

00:03:30.569 --> 00:03:35.919
from the moment we meet him, yet seeing him
finally meet his tragic end still hurts.

00:03:35.919 --> 00:03:42.260
Jordan's had some sizable performances before,
of course, but this one is absolutely unforgettable.

00:03:42.260 --> 00:03:44.079
Besting the braggadocio

00:03:44.079 --> 00:03:48.500
It's always incredible when a young actor
manages to not only hold their own in a film

00:03:48.500 --> 00:03:53.319
featuring a slew of major movie stars, but
manages to come out as the standout performer

00:03:53.319 --> 00:03:54.499
in the film.

00:03:54.499 --> 00:03:59.040
There are few better examples of this than
Hailee Steinfeld's turn in the Coen Brothers'

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:01.269
remake of True Grit.

00:04:01.269 --> 00:04:06.889
Steinfeld scored the role of Mattie Ross from
a pool of 15,000 actresses, and she was just

00:04:06.889 --> 00:04:11.099
13 when she began filming on what was her
first major movie role.

00:04:11.099 --> 00:04:15.900
True Grit tells the story of a young girl
who hires a drunk US Marshal named Rooster

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:19.840
Cogburn to help her track down the man who
killed her father.

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:25.380
And while Steinfeld acts alongside heavy-hitters
like Jeff Bridges, Josh Brolin, and Matt Damon

00:04:25.380 --> 00:04:28.700
in the movie, she steals every scene she's
in.

00:04:28.700 --> 00:04:33.700
Her take on Ross is snappy, angry, and fierce,
with Steinfeld channeling the character's

00:04:33.700 --> 00:04:38.450
laser-like focus on vengeance with the kind
of poise most actors four times her age aren't

00:04:38.450 --> 00:04:40.160
capable of portraying.

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:45.330
Some stars go their entire lives without turning
in a performance as authentic as the one Steinfeld

00:04:45.330 --> 00:04:48.620
crafted, and she did it on her feature film
debut.

00:04:48.620 --> 00:04:51.990
It doesn't get much more impressive than that.

00:04:51.990 --> 00:04:53.760
Humanizing the ape

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:58.130
Motion capture performances are becoming just
as impressive and important as live-action

00:04:58.130 --> 00:05:03.190
ones, and there's no one better at putting
on a high-tech computer suit than Andy Serkis.

00:05:03.190 --> 00:05:08.340
The actor has portrayed quite a few CGI-enhanced
characters of note, including Gollum from

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:13.400
The Lord of the Rings trilogy, Supreme Leader
Snoke from the new Star Wars trilogy, and

00:05:13.400 --> 00:05:15.730
Caesar from the Planet of the Apes trilogy.

00:05:15.730 --> 00:05:19.980
And he really shines in that last role as
the simian soldier.

00:05:19.980 --> 00:05:24.110
Not only is his performance in the role among
the best of his career, but it may just be

00:05:24.110 --> 00:05:27.720
the most important motion capture acting of
all time.

00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:31.490
Throughout the films, we see Caesar evolve
over the course of several years.

00:05:31.490 --> 00:05:36.500
In the beginning, he's a sweet, loving primate
whose intelligence is obvious but who's otherwise

00:05:36.500 --> 00:05:37.720
pretty innocent.

00:05:37.720 --> 00:05:42.580
By the time we get to the last film, however,
he's soulful, resonant, and all-too-human.

00:05:42.580 --> 00:05:46.150
"I came for you..."

00:05:46.150 --> 00:05:51.720
Serkis takes the character on a journey from
a young ape confused by his place in the world

00:05:51.720 --> 00:05:56.850
to a grizzled, unbreakable leader of a new
nation, and his performance never once falters

00:05:56.850 --> 00:05:58.130
along the way.

00:05:58.130 --> 00:06:02.910
Yes, the special effects crew does a stellar
job in transferring that performance to animation,

00:06:02.910 --> 00:06:08.310
but at its core Serkis' tremendous work proves
that he's a true pioneer in motion capture

00:06:08.310 --> 00:06:09.460
acting.

00:06:09.460 --> 00:06:10.460
Matching the tempo

00:06:10.460 --> 00:06:14.540
J.K. Simmons has pretty much always been impressive.

00:06:14.540 --> 00:06:19.360
From his gritty work on the small screen to
his memorable foray into the superhero realm

00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:23.220
to his versatile film appearances, he doesn't
seem to miss.

00:06:23.220 --> 00:06:26.090
Even his insurance company commercials are
worth watching.

00:06:26.090 --> 00:06:30.580
But Simmons' magnum opus is his role as Terence
Fletcher in Whiplash.

00:06:30.580 --> 00:06:35.770
In the film, he portrays the jazz professor
from hell -- a maniacally focused and demanding

00:06:35.770 --> 00:06:40.690
conductor who berates and abuses his students
until they either achieve brilliance or go

00:06:40.690 --> 00:06:41.690
insane.

00:06:41.690 --> 00:06:46.890
His simultaneous mentorship and relentless
antagonism of Miles Teller's Andrew is both

00:06:46.890 --> 00:06:49.530
engrossing and repulsive.

00:06:49.530 --> 00:06:54.180
Simmons strikes the perfect balance of mania,
never going so over the top as to ruin the

00:06:54.180 --> 00:06:56.370
audience's suspension of disbelief.

00:06:56.370 --> 00:07:00.470
And yet there's a humanity Simmons gives to
Fletcher when he easily could have been a

00:07:00.470 --> 00:07:02.190
cartoonish monster.

00:07:02.190 --> 00:07:07.390
There are plenty of great iconic movie antagonists,
but Fletcher's realism makes for one that

00:07:07.390 --> 00:07:11.260
will haunt any young artist's deepest insecurities.

00:07:11.260 --> 00:07:12.870
Elevating the material

00:07:12.870 --> 00:07:17.000
It goes without saying, but Meryl Streep is
one of the greatest actors of all time.

00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:21.660
Her filmography contains a multitude of flawless
performances, which makes picking just one

00:07:21.660 --> 00:07:24.240
sublime showcase next to impossible.

00:07:24.240 --> 00:07:29.200
That said, there is one character that simply
would not have worked without her, and that's

00:07:29.200 --> 00:07:32.070
Miranda Priestly in The Devil Wears Prada.

00:07:32.070 --> 00:07:36.470
The film is based on the novel of the same
name, which tells the story of a college graduate

00:07:36.470 --> 00:07:41.980
working as the personal assistant to a tyrannical
editor-in-chief of a fashion magazine.

00:07:41.980 --> 00:07:46.300
The film, and her role as Miranda Priestly,
should have been a relatively forgettable

00:07:46.300 --> 00:07:51.030
one, but with Streep coming in and bringing
her A-game to the picture, the whole movie

00:07:51.030 --> 00:07:56.370
is lifted from simple dramedy status to an
absolute must-see that'll stand the test of

00:07:56.370 --> 00:07:57.370
time.

00:07:57.370 --> 00:08:02.570
Streep's infinitely quotable ice queen bears
an unwavering facade of steely perfection.

00:08:02.570 --> 00:08:07.111
Yet she lets that mask slip just long enough
to allow our young heroine, and the audience

00:08:07.111 --> 00:08:11.730
by extension, see the humanity that lies beneath
all that brass.

00:08:11.730 --> 00:08:17.030
It's one thing to act well in an already well-crafted
film but it takes a truly magnificent performer

00:08:17.030 --> 00:08:21.030
to turn a role like Priestly into an Oscar-nominated
performance.

00:08:21.030 --> 00:08:25.900
If Streep's screen talents weren't already
documented enough, her work in The Devil Wears

00:08:25.900 --> 00:08:31.120
Prada proves that there's simply no one as
good or as effective as her.

00:08:31.120 --> 00:08:32.380
Being a BAMF

00:08:32.380 --> 00:08:36.079
There's very little to say about Pulp Fiction
that hasn't already been said.

00:08:36.079 --> 00:08:41.439
Quentin Tarantino's masterclass in filmmaking
is arguably the most quotable movie of a generation,

00:08:41.439 --> 00:08:46.649
and most of the best lines came from Samuel
L. Jackson's Jules, a righteous hitman who

00:08:46.649 --> 00:08:51.160
finds himself in the midst of an existential
crisis over the course of the film.

00:08:51.160 --> 00:08:55.720
Jackson is extremely prolific as an actor
and has appeared in a number of Tarantino

00:08:55.720 --> 00:09:01.410
films, but it's his turn here that stands
out as one of his finest hours as an actor.

00:09:01.410 --> 00:09:06.339
There's a calm, controlled intensity to Jules
that lingers throughout Jackson's performance.

00:09:06.339 --> 00:09:11.240
He calculates everything and almost always
has the upper hand, which is why his near-death

00:09:11.240 --> 00:09:14.720
experience in the film shakes him to his core.

00:09:14.720 --> 00:09:19.180
Jackson makes this experience less an unraveling
and more a crossroads for the character.

00:09:19.180 --> 00:09:24.329
It makes his encounter with petty criminals
Ringo and Yolanda all the more riveting and

00:09:24.329 --> 00:09:25.329
pivotal.

00:09:25.329 --> 00:09:29.320
Consider the controlled rage and desperation
that goes into every line:

00:09:29.320 --> 00:09:30.800
"And what's Fonzie like?

00:09:32.140 --> 00:09:34.000
Come on Yolanda what's Fonzie like?"

00:09:34.080 --> 00:09:34.780
"He's cool."

00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:35.520
"What?!"

00:09:35.520 --> 00:09:36.380
"Cool."

00:09:36.380 --> 00:09:37.380
"Correctamundo."

00:09:38.540 --> 00:09:43.080
In a film filled with memorable characters
and performances, Jackson's stands out as

00:09:43.080 --> 00:09:47.740
the definitive performance in one of the great
modern American films.

00:09:47.740 --> 00:09:49.060
Channeling the insanity

00:09:49.420 --> 00:09:54.319
Films based on Stephen King books provide
countless stellar villains to choose from,

00:09:54.319 --> 00:09:58.439
from Jack Nicholson's Jack Torrance in The
Shining to Pennywise from It.

00:09:58.439 --> 00:10:03.670
However, there's one that seems to stand out
from the rest, even all these years later:

00:10:03.670 --> 00:10:08.019
Kathy Bates' Oscar-winning turn as Annie Wilkes
Misery.

00:10:08.019 --> 00:10:12.809
Wilkes is fan culture gone toxic — well,
more toxic than it already is.

00:10:12.809 --> 00:10:17.740
Her attachment to writer Paul Sheldon's novels
is fanatical, leading her to kidnap him after

00:10:17.740 --> 00:10:19.339
a car accident.

00:10:19.339 --> 00:10:23.459
Oftentimes when a villain is so memorable,
it's because the actor brings a relatability

00:10:23.459 --> 00:10:25.209
one wouldn't expect to the role.

00:10:25.209 --> 00:10:27.779
But Wilkes is just not that kind of woman.

00:10:27.779 --> 00:10:32.529
Her performance is of someone so unhinged
and unpredictable that it frightens you to

00:10:32.529 --> 00:10:33.529
your core.

00:10:33.529 --> 00:10:36.560
"What do you think I say when I go to the
feed store in town?"

00:10:36.560 --> 00:10:42.220
"Oh now, Wally, get me a bag of that effing
pig feet and 10lbs of that beastly cow corn!"

00:10:43.580 --> 00:10:48.440
Bates plays Wilkes as a woman who truly believes
her behavior is normal, which makes it all

00:10:48.440 --> 00:10:51.920
the more horrifying when she lashes out at
poor Paul Sheldon.

00:10:51.920 --> 00:10:57.959
Sure, it's the leg-smashing scene that really
got everyone talking, but it's Bates' full-package

00:10:57.960 --> 00:11:01.320
villainy that rattled everyone's nerves for
the long haul.

00:11:01.320 --> 00:11:04.920
"Is this what you're looking for?"

00:11:05.280 --> 00:11:06.279
Thanks for watching!

00:11:06.279 --> 00:11:08.990
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:11:08.990 --> 00:11:11.319
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

